From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix Net4x.Gtk.Windows.Forms defects found by functional tests

Behavioural defects surfaced by the Net4x.Gtk.Windows.Forms.Tests functional suite.

Control
 - Right and Bottom reported the widget's end/bottom *margins* instead of the control's edges
 - HasChildren and TopLevelControl were auto-properties nothing ever assigned, so they were always
   false/null; Contains() always returned false
 - DataBindings was an auto-property nothing ever assigned, so it was null on every control that did not
   override it and data binding could not be used at all
 - ClientRectangle reported the GTK allocation origin, so an unrealized control returned -1,-1
 - GetChildAtPoint always returned null and GetContainerControl only ever looked at the control itself
 - the three Scale overloads and the whole Reset* family were empty method bodies
 - SetBounds(x, y, w, h, BoundsSpecified) started from the GTK allocation, which is meaningless before
   the control is realized, so the parts meant to be preserved were replaced with garbage
 - GetChildAtPointSkip was an empty class where WinForms defines a flags enum, so the skip argument
   could not express anything
 - ControlCollection.Add duplicated a control that was added twice and left a re-parented control in
   its previous parent's collection
 - ControlCollection.Find ignored searchAllChildren; SetChildIndex had an empty body and never reordered

Form
 - TopMost reported whether the window was *active* rather than whether it was kept above other windows
 - Opacity read back GTK's 8-bit quantisation (0.502 for 0.5) instead of the value that was assigned

DataGridView
 - the RowCount setter had its loop inverted, so growing the grid spun forever removing rows until
   RemoveAt(-1) threw; it now adds and removes rows to reach the requested count
 - DataGridViewRowCollection.AddCopies passed the source index to ArrayList.CopyTo as the destination
   offset (throwing for any non-zero index) and appended the same row instances instead of copies
 - Add(int count) returned one past the index of the last row added
 - RemoveAt accepted a negative index and threw from deep inside ArrayList
 - DataGridViewColumn.AutoSizeMode only stored its backing field in the None branch, so every other mode
   was dropped and the property always read back NotSet

Data binding
 - BindingSource.Dispose cleared the caller's own list, destroying the bound data; on a DataView-backed
   source it threw "Cannot clear this list" outright
 - BindingContext was missing the WinForms this[dataSource] / this[dataSource, dataMember] indexers and
   the single-argument Contains

ListView / ListBox / TreeView
 - ListView.Clear left the columns behind; both FindItemWithText overloads ignored their
   includeSubItemsInSearch and isPrefixSearch flags
 - ListViewItem built from a string[] duplicated the first entry, leaving one sub-item too many
 - ListBox.SelectionMode never assigned its backing field, so the getter always reported the initial mode
 - ListBox.CustomTabOffsets was an always-null auto-property
 - TreeView.ShowLines ignored its argument and always switched lines on
 - TreeNodeCollection was missing the WinForms Add(string), Add(key, text), this[key], IndexOfKey and
   ContainsKey members, so ordinary WinForms code did not compile against the shim

ToolStrip
 - ToolStripMenuItem.Checked drove the GTK check-indicator visibility while CheckState drove the actual
   toggle, so the two never agreed
 - the WinForms ToolStripMenuItem(text), (text, image) and (text, image, onClick) constructors were
   missing

ToolTip
 - SetToolTip used Dictionary.Add, so giving a control a second caption threw; it now replaces, applies
   the caption to the widget, and clears the tooltip when the caption is null or empty
 - the InitialDelay setter overwrote all four delays, silently resetting AutomaticDelay, AutoPopDelay and
   ReshowDelay; AutomaticDelay now derives the other three the way WinForms does

ImageList
 - image keys were matched case-sensitively; WinForms matches them case-insensitively

Threading (the highest-risk gap in EMULATION-GAPS.md)
 - Control.Invoke queued the delegate on the GLib idle loop and returned immediately, so it always
   returned null and gave no ordering guarantee; it now runs inline on the owning thread or posts to the
   main loop and waits, rethrowing the delegate's own exception on the caller
 - InvokeRequired was an auto-property with no backing logic, so it was permanently false and the
   standard "if (InvokeRequired) Invoke(...)" idiom silently ran UI code on worker threads; it now
   compares against the thread that created the control
 - BeginInvoke dispatched to the thread pool - the one place WinForms guarantees the delegate will not
   run - and now posts to the GTK main loop

ToolStripItem
 - Width and Height were auto-properties disconnected from Size, so setting one left the other stale
 - Checked and CheckState were independent auto-properties and never agreed
 - SetBounds(Rectangle, BoundsSpecified) threw NotImplementedException
 - the WinForms Margin member was commented out, so setting an item's margin did not compile

ResXDataNode
 - a null name reached name.Length and produced a NullReferenceException instead of the documented
   ArgumentNullException

Data binding never delivered a value to a control (four chained gaps)
 - Control.DataBindings and Control.BindingContext were auto-properties nothing ever assigned, so no
   binding manager was ever created for a control
 - Control.Created was only set by PerformLayout/CreateControl, so a live control with a real GTK handle
   reported itself uncreated - and a Binding only activates for a created component
 - Binding.CheckBinding never finished with UpdateIsBinding(), so a binding attached after its manager
   already existed stayed inactive and PushData wrote null into the bound control property
 - BindingSource.Filter left the cursor past the end of the filtered list

Emulation gaps from EMULATION-GAPS.md
 - ListView.VirtualMode was spelled VirtualModel, so designer-generated code from a real WinForms project
   did not compile; the old spelling is kept as an obsolete alias (SS2.12)
 - SetStyle(ControlStyles, bool) was an empty method and there was no GetStyle at all, so the standard
   custom-control recipe recorded nothing and could not be read back (SS2.4)
 - Control.DrawToBitmap was an empty method, so callers got a blank image with no error (SS2.14)
 - PrintDialog.RunPrint ignored its showDialog argument and PrintDocument.Print() always showed the print
   dialog, so unattended printing blocked on a UI prompt (SS2.10)

Class A types that did not exist at all (EMULATION-GAPS.md SS3.1)
 - TextRenderer (MeasureText/DrawText, the recommended WinForms text API since .NET 2.0 and the most-hit
   compile break), measuring and drawing through Graphics with the alignment, single-line and padding
   bits of TextFormatFlags honoured
 - ControlPaint (Light/Dark/LightLight/DarkDark, DrawBorder, DrawBorder3D, DrawFocusRectangle,
   DrawCheckBox, DrawButton, FillReversibleRectangle, DisabledColor)
 - SystemInformation (border/scrollbar/caption/icon metrics, monitor geometry, environment strings)
 - ButtonBorderStyle, which ControlPaint's border overloads need

 - Clipboard/DataObject GetDataPresent (x3) and GetFormats (x2) all threw, so the guard every well-written
   paste handler calls before GetData failed while the unguarded call worked (SS4.2)

AutoScaleMode (SS2.13) - API implemented, automatic application deliberately withheld
 - AutoScaleMode and AutoScaleDimensions were inert auto-properties duplicated on Form and UserControl;
   they now live on ContainerControl where WinForms puts them, joined by CurrentAutoScaleDimensions,
   AutoScaleFactor and a working PerformAutoScale()
 - scaling is NOT applied automatically when layout resumes: a designer-generated form carries
   AutoScaleDimensions baked in by the Windows designer, so the ratio against a Pango-measured font is the
   difference between two typefaces rather than a change of environment. Applying it shrank the sample
   application to about 79% while leaving the window size untouched, clipping captions and wrapping labels
 - Form.ResumeLayout was an empty override that swallowed the base implementation entirely

LinkLabel (SS4.3 listed it at 24 throw sites, the highest of any control)
 - Links was an auto-property nothing ever assigned, so it was null and every use threw a
   NullReferenceException before reaching the unimplemented members
 - LinkCollection had no backing store: Add overwrote the owner's Text with the value's string form and
   returned a hard-coded 1, Clear wiped the Text, Contains did a substring search on it, and Count, the
   indexer, IndexOf, Insert, Remove, RemoveAt, CopyTo, GetEnumerator and the four ICollection/IList state
   properties all threw. It is now a real ordered collection with keyed lookup
 - Link discarded its constructor arguments and had no Start, Length, Enabled, Visited, Name or Tag, so a
   link could not describe the span of text it covered
 - a multi-link LinkLabel - the reason the control exists over a plain Label - is now expressible

The legacy menu and status API (SS3.5), which was missing entirely
 - Menu, MenuItem, MainMenu, ContextMenu, StatusBar, StatusBarPanel, the Shortcut enum and the
   StatusBarPanel* enums/event args. These are obsolete in modern WinForms but are exactly what an
   application old enough to need porting uses, so their absence was a compile break at the very start of
   a migration rather than a runtime surprise later
 - implemented as adapters over the ToolStrip family, which is what renders: MainMenu drives a MenuStrip,
   MenuItem a ToolStripMenuItem, ContextMenu a ContextMenuStrip, StatusBarPanel a ToolStripStatusLabel;
   StatusBar derives from StatusStrip so it is a real dockable control
 - Form.Menu and Control.ContextMenu attach them, and also set MainMenuStrip/ContextMenuStrip so legacy and
   modern menu code share one attachment point
 - ToolStripItemCollection had an unqualified 'Menu' field that bound to Gtk.Menu; it is now qualified,
   because the WinForms Menu type exists in that namespace again

 - ErrorProvider was missing: the standard WinForms validation pattern, so almost any data-entry form
   failed to compile. Implemented as an extender of the same shape as ToolTip; the error text is stored,
   reported by GetError and surfaced on the control. The adjacent blinking icon WinForms draws is not
   reproduced - placing a floating adornment beside an arbitrary child needs layout surgery in the GTK
   container model - but Icon, BlinkStyle, alignment and padding round-trip so designer output survives
 - Splitter, the legacy splitter that predates SplitContainer, was missing. It docks to an edge and
   resizes the sibling docked to the same edge, honouring MinSize/MinExtra and raising SplitterMoving and
   SplitterMoved. Interactive dragging is not wired to GTK input; setting SplitPosition performs the resize
 - the ErrorIconAlignment and ErrorBlinkStyle enumerations (SS3.4)

TabControl and ListBox throw sites (SS4.3)
 - TabPageCollection's public surface worked but every explicit IList/ICollection member threw, so any
   code reaching the pages through the interface - IList list = tabControl.TabPages, a CopyTo, a
   non-generic enumeration helper - failed. They now delegate to the public members
 - ListBox.FindString, FindString(s, startIndex), FindStringExact and FindStringExact(s, startIndex) all
   threw; they now search the items case-insensitively, wrapping around the end of the list, and return
   NoMatches for an absent or empty search string
 - ListBox.DrawMode threw from both accessors
 - ListBox.IndexFromPoint(Point) threw, and the int overload behind it dereferenced the null that
   GetRowAtY returns when the point is past the last item, instead of reporting no match

 - ToolStripItem.Bounds, DisplayRectangle and GetPreferredSize threw, so owner-draw code could not ask an
   item where it is or how big it wants to be

 - DataGridView data binding was deferred until the GTK widget was realized, so an unbound grid read
   Rows.Count == 0 immediately after DataSource was assigned - a deviation from WinForms, where the model
   is populated at assignment. OnSetDataSource only touches the column/row model and clears rows first, so
   it now runs on assignment and again (idempotently) on realize

